n)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nXL-MS data of trigger factor and GAPDH binding\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nWe present XL-MS data of the trigger factor:GAPDH complex to define the interaction sites for GAPDH on trigger factor.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe crosslinked complex was precipitated using chloroform–methanol, as previously described. Briefly, methanol (150\u2009μl) and chloroform (50\u2009μl) were added to a sample of the crosslinked proteins (50\u2009μl, 6\u2009μM). After vortexing to mix (30 s), water (100\u2009μl) was added. The solution was mixed again (by vortexing for 30 s) before being centrifuged (10,000\u2009g, 2\u2009min). The upper layer of aqueous solvent was removed and methanol (150\u2009μl) was added. The solution was mixed (by vortexing for 30 s) and centrifuged (10,000\u2009g, 2\u2009min), after which the supernatant was removed and the precipitate was air dried by placing in a laminar flow hood. The protein pellet was resuspended in 1 % (w/v) rapigest (Waters), 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate pH 8 (10 μl). Dithiothreitol (50 mM in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate pH 8, 10 μl) was then added and the mixture was incubated at 37 ºC for 1 h. Iodoacetamide (100 mM in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate pH 8, 10 μl) was then added and the mixture was incubated at 37 ºC for a further 1 h. Subsequently, trypsin was added (1:50 w/w enzyme:protein, Promega) along with 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate pH 8 (70 μl) and the mixture was incubated overnight at 37 ºC. The resultant peptides were desalted using Sep-Pak tC18 cartridges (Waters), according to the manufacturers instructions. The desalted peptides were evaporated to dryness, and resuspended in 0.1 % trifluoroethanol (50\u2009μl). The peptides were then analysed by liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS) on an Orbitrap Exploris 240 mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher).  Peptides (3 μl) were injected onto a 30 cm capillary emitter column (inner diameter 75 μm, packed with 3-μm Reprosil-Pur 120 C18 media, Dr Maisch) prepared in-house, and separated by gradient elution of 2-30 % (v/v) solvent B (0.1% (v/v) formic acid in acetonitrile) in solvent A (0.1% (v/v) formic acid in water) over 60\u2009min at 300\u2009nL.min−1. The mass spectrometer was operated in data dependent acquisition mode with precursor fragmentation performed by Higher-energy C-trap dissociation (HCD). Each high resolution scan (m/z range 500–1500, R\u2009=\u200960,000) was followed by 10 product ion scans (R\u2009=\u200915,000), using stepped normalised collision energies of 27, 30 and 33 %. Only precursor ions with charge states 3-7+ (inclusive) were selected for tandem MS. The dynamic exclusion was set to 60 s.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
